{"chunk_id": "ef1e3e3604da", "text": "Krishna explains the doctrine of the three gunas\u2014Goodness (sattva), Passion (rajas), and Darkness (tamas)\u2014which bind the embodied soul to worldly existence. Goodness illuminates and produces knowledge and happiness; Passion arises from desire and drives restless action; Darkness springs from ignorance and leads to delusion, inertia, and error. A person\u2019s dominant quality shapes their behavior, destiny, and rebirth. The wise recognize that these qualities alone act, and by detaching from them they transcend birth and suffering. Krishna then describes the person beyond the gunas: equanimous, impartial to praise or blame, pleasure or pain. The chapter continues with the cosmic Asvattha tree metaphor of worldly existence and culminates in Krishna revealing himself as Purushottama, the Supreme Being sustaining all life and the ultimate object of knowledge.\nKrishna explains the three gunas, the state beyond them, and the cosmic Ashvattha tree symbolizing worldly existence and the path to the Supreme.\nThemes: three gunas, transcending qualities, cosmic tree, nature of the soul, Supreme Being (Purushottama)", "vector": [0.0035971368197351694, 0.0061959936283528805, 0.008512004278600216, -0.012318779714405537, -0.03287670016288757, 0.007174307946115732, -0.024850524961948395, -0.01803559809923172, -0.03346235677599907, -0.011739777401089668, 0.015120619907975197, 0.008139312267303467, -0.005646939389407635, 0.020245125517249107, 0.007174307946115732, 0.008991178125143051, 0.04450999200344086, -0.01409571897238493, 0.011679880321025848, -0.005686870310455561, 0.013170645572245121, 0.0005656922003254294, -0.010262321680784225, -0.0071410322561860085, -0.012531746178865433, 0.01271809171885252, 0.005077919457107782, -0.02888357825577259, -0.002450778381898999, -0.014774549752473831, -0.004715210758149624, -0.006379011552780867, -0.01650490239262581, 0.004934832453727722, -0.020324988290667534, 0.006062889471650124, -0.00017